## JSON Serialization with `System.Text.Json` – a quick‑start guide

`System.Text.Json` is the modern, high‑performance JSON library that ships with .NET Core 3.0+ and .NET 5/6/7/8.  
It’s built into the runtime, has no external dependencies, and offers a clean, attribute‑driven API that is very similar to the older `Newtonsoft.Json` (`Json.NET`) but usually faster and smaller.

Below you’ll find a step‑by‑step walk‑through of the basics:

| Topic | What it is | Where it comes in |
|-------|------------|-------------------|
| `JsonSerializer.Serialize` | Turns a .NET object into a JSON string (or writes it to a stream) | **Writing** |
| `JsonSerializer.Deserialize` | Turns a JSON string (or stream) back into a .NET object | **Reading** |
| `JsonSerializerOptions` | Configure naming policy, indentation, converters, etc. | Optional, but useful |
| `[JsonPropertyName]`, `[JsonIgnore]` | Fine‑grained control on a per‑property basis | Attribute usage |
| Custom converters | Serialize/deserialize types that don’t map cleanly (e.g., enums, dates) | `JsonConverter<T>` |

---

## 1. A minimal example

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization;

// 1. Define a simple POCO
public class Person
{
    public string FirstName { get; set; }
    public string LastName  { get; set; }
    public int    Age       { get; set; }

    // Optional: rename a property in JSON
    [JsonPropertyName("emailAddress")]
    public string Email { get; set; }

    // Optional: exclude from JSON
    [JsonIgnore]
    public string Password { get; set; }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // 2. Create an object
        var person = new Person
        {
            FirstName = "Ada",
            LastName  = "Lovelace",
            Age       = 36,
            Email     = "ada@example.com",
            Password  = "superSecret"
        };

        // 3. Serialize to JSON string (pretty‑printed)
        var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
        {
            WriteIndented = true,                     // human‑readable
            PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase  // camelCase keys
        };

        string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(person, options);
        Console.WriteLine("Serialized JSON:");
        Console.WriteLine(json);

        // 4. Deserialize back to a Person object
        Person deserialized = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Person>(json, options);
        Console.WriteLine("\nDeserialized Person:");
        Console.WriteLine($"{deserialized.FirstName} {deserialized.LastName}, Age {deserialized.Age}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Email: {deserialized.Email}");
        // Password will be null because it was ignored during serialization
    }
}
```

**Output**

```json
Serialized JSON:
{
  "firstName": "Ada",
  "lastName": "Lovelace",
  "age": 36,
  "emailAddress": "ada@example.com"
}

Deserialized Person:
Ada Lovelace, Age 36
Email: ada@example.com
```

### Why did we see `emailAddress` instead of `Email`?

`[JsonPropertyName("emailAddress")]` tells the serializer to use that exact key in the JSON payload.  
Because we set `PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase`, the default naming policy would normally produce `email`, but the attribute overrides it.

---

## 2. Common “gotchas” and advanced topics

### 2.1 Handling `null` values

```csharp
var opt = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    DefaultIgnoreCondition = JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull
};
```

This will omit any property that is `null` from the output.

### 2.2 Including private fields

```csharp
options.IncludeFields = true;
```

The default is *no* – only public properties are serialized. Set this if you want to expose fields.

### 2.3 Serializing enums as strings

```csharp
public enum Status { Pending, Active, Suspended }

public class Account
{
    public string Name   { get; set; }
    public Status State { get; set; }
}

// Use JsonStringEnumConverter
options.Converters.Add(new JsonStringEnumConverter());
```

Without the converter, enums are written as numbers (`0`, `1`, …).

### 2.4 Custom converters

Suppose you want to serialize a `DateTime` as an ISO‑8601 string but *only* if it’s non‑`DateTime.MinValue`:

```csharp
public class NonDefaultDateTimeConverter : JsonConverter<DateTime>
{
    public override DateTime Read(ref Utf8JsonReader reader, Type typeToConvert, JsonSerializerOptions options)
        => reader.GetDateTime();

    public override void Write(Utf8JsonWriter writer, DateTime value, JsonSerializerOptions options)
    {
        if (value == DateTime.MinValue)
            writer.WriteNullValue();
        else
            writer.WriteStringValue(value.ToString("o")); // ISO 8601
    }
}

// Add it to options
options.Converters.Add(new NonDefaultDateTimeConverter());
```

---

## 3. Working with streams (files, network, etc.)

### 3.1 Writing to a file

```csharp
await File.WriteAllTextAsync("person.json", json);
```

### 3.2 Reading from a file

```csharp
string fileJson = await File.ReadAllTextAsync("person.json");
var personFromFile = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Person>(fileJson);
```

### 3.3 Using a `Stream` directly

```csharp
using var stream = File.Create("person.json");
await JsonSerializer.SerializeAsync(stream, person, options);
```

and

```csharp
using var stream = File.OpenRead("person.json");
var personFromStream = await JsonSerializer.DeserializeAsync<Person>(stream);
```

These async APIs are preferred for I/O‑bound operations.

---

## 4. Summary checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| ✅ | Use `System.Text.Json` (no NuGet needed in .NET 5+) |
| ✅ | `JsonSerializer.Serialize(obj, options)` → string/stream |
| ✅ | `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<T>(json, options)` → object |
| ✅ | Configure via `JsonSerializerOptions` (indentation, naming, converters) |
| ✅ | Use attributes (`[JsonPropertyName]`, `[JsonIgnore]`, etc.) for fine‑grained control |
| ✅ | Add custom converters for special types |
| ✅ | Prefer async stream APIs for file/network I/O |
| ✅ | `WriteIndented = true` for human‑readable JSON |

That’s it! With these basics you can serialize almost any C# object to JSON and deserialize it back, while controlling naming, formatting, and custom behaviors via options or attributes. Happy coding!
